= Reference — Technical =

== Semantic value ==
In extensional semantics, an expression may be assigned a denotation relative to an interpretation.

For a singular term t:

<pre>
⟦t⟧ᵢ = d
</pre>

where d is an entity in the interpretation's domain.

Intensional frameworks may relativize evaluation to worlds, times, assignments, contexts, or information states.

== Sense and reference ==
Fregean approaches distinguish cognitive/semantic mode of presentation from reference. Co-referring expressions can therefore differ in sense.

Russellian, direct-reference, causal-historical and descriptivist theories distribute semantic work differently across descriptions, referents and propositions.

== Context sensitivity ==
Indexicals and demonstratives require contextual parameters or acts of demonstration/intentional selection.

Anaphoric expressions can depend on linguistic discourse structure rather than only the physical situation.

This motivates separating:

<pre>
expression type
utterance token
context
referential act
referent
</pre>

== Formal identifier systems ==
IRIs, database keys and ontology identifiers can function as names in a formal system, but string equality and real-world co-reference are different questions.

A formal identity assertion such as <code>owl:sameAs</code> makes a strong model-theoretic commitment; lexical similarity does not.

